  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Given , <math><msup><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></math> and <math><msup><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></math><br /><br /><math><mo>&#8756;</mo><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>25</mn></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mi>&#160;</mi><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac></math><br /><br />So, the coordinates of foci <math><mi>S</mi></math> and <math><msup><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#8242;</mi></mrow></msup></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math> and <math><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math> respectively.</p><p>Let,&nbsp;<math><mi>P</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos</mi></mrow><mo>&#8289;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#952;</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin</mi></mrow><mo>&#8289;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#952;</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfenced></math> be a variable point on the ellipse.<br /><br />Then, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8710;</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>&#8242;</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mfenced open=\"|\" close=\"|\" separators=\"|\"><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>3</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>5</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#952;</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>4</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#952;</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#952;</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>&#160;</mi></math><br /><br />Since the value of <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin</mi></mrow><mo>&#8289;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#952;</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>&#160;</mi></math>lies between <math><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math><br /><br />So, the maximum value of area of <math><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>S</mi><msup><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#8242;</mi></mrow></msup></math> is <math><mn>12</mn></math>&nbsp;sq. units<br /><br />Also,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#8805;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>since</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>A.</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>&#8805;</mo><mi>G.</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#8805;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo></math></p>",
